# Audited false positives for check_secret_leak. Format: <path>[:<line>].
# A `#` comment is stripped, so every entry carries its reason above it.
#
# Line-pinned on purpose, and the trade-off is deliberate: an entry that drifts
# off its line stops matching and the gate REDS. That is the safe direction for
# a secret gate — someone re-audits and moves the pin — whereas a path-only
# entry would silently allow a real secret pasted anywhere in the file.

# gate-coverage.yml carries mutation-canary recipes, and this gate matches on
# CONTENT, so a canary that proves the gate can still SEE a private key has to
# contain a key-shaped string. The recipe below is the `check_secret_leak
# --pack` canary: a PEM header planted at a shipped-and-gitignored path
# (`dist/**`, which `files[]` ships and `.gitignore:206` ignores) to prove the
# pack scope reaches what no git-backed mode can. It is not a credential — the
# body reads `zz-canary-not-a-real-key` — and it is never written anywhere
# except by `check_gate_coverage --canary`, which deletes it in a `finally`.
#
# Its sibling `check_pack_size`'s canary needs NO entry here, because that
# gate's credential class matches on the PATH (`\.pem$`) and its recipe
# therefore carries no key-shaped body at all.
# RE-DERIVED 2026-08-24 after a merge re-pinned this twice in one day:
# 301 (chained-clip branch) and 306 (main, after the deterministic-time
# roadmap added manifest rows above the recipe) both described a tree that
# no longer exists. The merged file puts the recipe at 307, and the number
# is DERIVED from it rather than taken from either side — picking a side is
# how a line pin silently stops covering the line it was written for. The
# body was re-read before the pin moved: byte-identical, still
# `zz-canary-not-a-real-key`, still the gate's own planted canary.
# RE-PINNED 306 on 2026-08-24 (drain/road-to-score-contract). The 307 above was
# already wrong ON MAIN: `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY'` reads 306 at
# origin/main and 306 on the branch, so the previous re-derivation landed one
# line high and the entry has been covering nothing since. It stayed invisible
# because this gate is DIFF-SCOPED — nothing red until a change touches
# gate-coverage.yml, which is exactly the latent shape a line pin trades for
# safety. Working as designed, in the direction the header above calls safe.
# Body re-read before the pin moved, per this file's own contract: byte-identical,
# still `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`, still the gate's own planted canary.
# RE-PINNED 339 on 2026-08-24 (drain/road-to-command-surface-legibility). The
# `check_command_examples` manifest row landed ABOVE this recipe and pushed it
# down 33 lines, so 306 stopped covering it and CI red on `secret-vcs-guard` --
# the third time in one day this pin has moved, and each time for the same
# structural reason: the recipe sits near the END of a file that grows from the
# top. Derived from `grep -n 'BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY' src/config/gate-coverage.yml`
# on the merged tree, not adjusted by the +33 the diff happened to add. Body
# re-read before moving it, per the contract above: byte-identical, still
# `zz-canary-not-a-real-key-zz`.
#
# WORTH NAMING RATHER THAN RE-PINNING A FOURTH TIME: a line pin on a recipe
# inside an append-heavy manifest drifts on almost every change to that manifest.
# The header calls the drift direction safe and it is -- the gate reds rather
# than allowing. But the cost is a re-pin per manifest row, paid by whoever
# happens to add the next gate. Two shapes would end it and both are decisions
# rather than fixes: move canary recipes out of the manifest into per-gate files
# so the pin sits in a file nobody appends to, or teach `readAllowFile` a
# content-anchored form (`path:@<literal>`) so the entry follows the body it was
# audited against. Neither is done here; a drain run re-pinning a security
# suppression is not the place to redesign one.
src/config/gate-coverage.yml:339
